<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Gregory XI</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1329–<a href="/years/1378">1378</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Pierre Roger de Beaufort</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gregory XI was pope from 1370 to 1378, the last French pope. Urged by Catherine of Siena among others, he moved the papal court from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1377 to restore papal authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the Papal States. His death the next year was followed by the disputed election that began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Great Western Schism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xi.jpg" alt="Pope Gregory XI" width="252" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Virgil Master and his atelier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duke_of_Anjou_leading_Pope_Gregory_XI_to_the_palace_at_Avignon,_while_cardinals_follow_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vi">Pierre Roger</a> de Beaufort was born at Maumont in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His uncle, Pierre Cardinal Roger, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, was elected pope in 1342 as Clement VI. Clement gave his nephew a number of benefices and in 1348 made the eighteen-year-old a cardinal deacon. He studied at the University of Perugia, where he became a skilled canonist and theologian.</p><p>After the death of Pope Urban V in December 1370, eighteen cardinals entered the conclave at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> on 29 December. Cardinal Roger was unanimously elected the next day. He first opposed his own election but accepted and took the name Gregory XI. He was ordained priest on 4 January 1371, consecrated and crowned the following day in the cathedral of Notre Dame des Doms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Gregory tried at once to reconcile the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and failed. On 20 August 1372 he confirmed a treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> at Villeneuve-lès-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> that settled the dispute between those two papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>. He also tried to reform corrupt practices in the monastic orders, such as fees charged to visitors at holy sites and the display of false relics, and in 1376, at the urging of Catherine of Siena, he tried to organise a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>.</p><p>Like earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> popes he appointed Frenchmen as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legatus">legates</a> and governors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, whom the Italians hated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernabo-visconti">Bernabò Visconti</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> seized Reggio and other papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> in 1371, and Gregory excommunicated him. A Florentine-led coalition of Italian cities then fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> in the War of the Eight Saints (1375–1378); on 31 March 1376 Gregory excommunicated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and placed it under interdict. Catherine of Siena pleaded for peace on Florence's behalf, but the war ended only after his death, with a peace concluded at Tivoli in July 1378.</p><p>Catherine also wrote him 14 letters between 1375 and 1378 urging reform, peace and a return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Despite the protests of the French king and most of the cardinals, Gregory left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> on 13 September 1376, sailed from Marseille on 2 October, reached Corneto on 6 December and entered Rome on 17 January 1377, ending about 68 years of papal rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gregory XI died on 27 March 1378, aged 48 or 49, and was succeeded by the Italian Urban VI. Most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> then supported Clement VII, now considered an antipope, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Western Schism</a> that followed forced Europe into a dilemma of allegiance. It was not fully resolved until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> (1414–1418) and the election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-martin-v">Pope Martin V</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Gregory XI” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rosiers-d'Égletons, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.38,2.01&amp;year=1360" aria-label="Rosiers-d'Égletons on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4598 563 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Gregory XI"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5048 833h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5048 833h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.38,2.01&amp;year=1360">What was here in 1360? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
